## /etc/qubes/policy.d/30-hybrid-anon.policy
##
## Qubes 4.2+ policy format. Numeric prefix controls evaluation order:
## 30- is evaluated before the 90-default.policy shipped by Qubes, so these
## rules win. Do not edit 90-default.policy itself; it is package-owned and
## will be overwritten on update.
##
## Specifier used throughout: @dispvm:anon-dvm
##   Matches any disposable spawned from the anon-dvm template, including the
##   preloaded ones. Using a tag instead would be fragile — tag inheritance
##   onto unnamed disposables is not something to bet an anonymity boundary on.
##
## DESIGN NOTE — the spec asked for a "multi-factor dom0 prompt" before
## clipboard data could leave an anonymous disposable. No such mechanism
## exists. Qubes RPC policy has exactly three actions: allow, deny, ask.
## Building an MFA prompt would mean shipping new code into dom0, which is the
## last place in the system you want more code. What follows is the honest
## version of that intent: unconditional deny on the paths that matter, and a
## deliberate ask (with no default target pre-filled) everywhere else.

### ------------------------------------------------------------------
### Clipboard
### ------------------------------------------------------------------

## Nothing from an anonymous disposable reaches the vault. No prompt, because
## a prompt is a thing you can click through at 2am.
qubes.ClipboardPaste    *   @dispvm:anon-dvm    vault           deny

## Into any other persistent qube: ask, every time, no default_target. You
## have to consciously select the destination in the dom0 dialog.
qubes.ClipboardPaste    *   @dispvm:anon-dvm    @anyvm          ask

## Pasting *into* an anonymous disposable is also worth gating — it is the
## classic deanonymisation vector (pasting a real name, a real address, a
## logged-in session token into a Tor session).
qubes.ClipboardPaste    *   @anyvm              @dispvm:anon-dvm    ask

### ------------------------------------------------------------------
### File movement
### ------------------------------------------------------------------

## No file ever leaves an anonymous disposable automatically. If you genuinely
## need a file out, you are doing something that deserves a conscious manual
## act, not a keybinding.
qubes.Filecopy          *   @dispvm:anon-dvm    @anyvm          deny

## Files into a disposable are fine — that is the whole "open this untrusted
## PDF somewhere it cannot hurt me" workflow.
qubes.Filecopy          *   @anyvm              @dispvm:anon-dvm    allow

### ------------------------------------------------------------------
### Open-in-VM / URL handoff
### ------------------------------------------------------------------

## 4.3 already blocks files and URLs from Whonix-Workstation qubes being
## opened in a *non-Whonix* disposable (qubes-issues#10051). These lines cover
## the inverse direction and the named-qube case, which that fix does not.

## An anonymous disposable may not hand a file or URL to a persistent qube.
## That is the single easiest way to correlate a Tor session with a clearnet
## identity: "open this in my normal browser".
qubes.OpenInVM          *   @dispvm:anon-dvm    @anyvm          deny
qubes.OpenURL           *   @dispvm:anon-dvm    @anyvm          deny

## Sending a URL *into* a fresh anonymous disposable is the good direction.
qubes.OpenURL           *   @anyvm              @dispvm:anon-dvm    allow

### ------------------------------------------------------------------
### Admin API
### ------------------------------------------------------------------

## Explicit: an anonymous disposable has no business talking to qubesd.
## (Default is deny, but an anonymity boundary should state its own
## assumptions rather than inherit them.)
admin.*                 *   @dispvm:anon-dvm    @anyvm          deny
